ute (year -4) % 12, then take that index.\n\nLet's test 2020: (2020-4) = 2016. 2016 %12 is 0. So index 0 is Rat. Correct.\n\nAnother test: 1924. (1924-4)=1920. 1920 mod 12 is 0. So again Rat. Correct. Then 1925 would be 1, which is Ox. That makes sense.\n\nSo the function would take the year, compute the remainder, then index into the list.\n\nNow, the function should return the animal as a string. So I need to create the list in order, then compute the index as (year -4) % 12, and return the corresponding animal.\n\nNow, considering possible edge cases. What if the year is 0? Probably, the function should handle all integers, but since the problem says input is a positive integer, maybe that's handled.\n\nNow, implementing this in Python.\n\nThe code would be:\n\ndef chinese_zodiac(year):\n    animals = ['Rat', 'Ox', 'Tiger', 'Rabbit', 'Dragon', 'Snake', 'Horse', 'Goat', 'Monkey', 'Rooster', 'Dog', 'Pig']\n    index = (year - 4) % 12\n    return animals[index]\n\nWait, but wait, let me check for another known year. 2021 is Ox? 2021-4=2017, mod12 is 1. Yes, Ox is index 1. Correct.\n\nSo that's the function.\n\nNow, the user also wants unit tests. So I need to create a separate test file, perhaps in experiments/test_solution.py. The tests should cover various cases, including the known years.\n\nFor example:\n\nimport unittest\n\nclass TestChineseZodiac(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_known_years(self):\n        self.assertEqual(chinese_zodiac(2020), 'Rat')\n        self.assertEqual(chinese_zodiac(2021), 'Ox')\n        self.assertEqual(chinese_zodiac(1924), 'Rat')\n        self.assertEqual(chinese_zodiac(1925), 'Ox')\n        self.assertEqual(chinese_zodiac(2000), 'Dragon')  # 2000-4=1996, mod12 is 1996/12= 166*12=1992, 1996-1992=4. 4 is Dragon. Correct.\n        self.assertEqual(chinese_zodiac(1996), 'Dragon')  # same as above\n        self.assertEqual(chinese_zodiac(2012), 'Dragon')  # 2012-4=2008 mod12 is 2008/12= 167*12=2004, 2008-2004=4. So same as above.\n\nW